micro_lesson.content:
Hashing is ideal when you need fast lookups, insertions, or deletions by key. Compare a nested-loop approach with a hash-based approach:

```python
# Nested loops: O(n^2)
def has_duplicate_nested(items):
    for i in range(len(items)):
        for j in range(i + 1, len(items)):
            if items[i] == items[j]:
                return True
    return False

# Hashing: O(n) average
def has_duplicate_hash(items):
    seen = set()
    for item in items:
        if item in seen:
            return True
        seen.add(item)
    return False
```

Use hashing when the dataset is large and membership tests or key-based retrieval are frequent. Avoid it when data is tiny, order matters and must be preserved without extra structures, or when hash collisions would make correctness harder to guarantee.

